      ":question/id": "e47c91b1-8ecd-5f80-b619-43c04e45be4b",
      ":question/problem": "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x+2y}{x-y+1}$.",
      ":question/requires-calculator": false,
      ":question/worked-solution": "The numerator is a polynomial, so it imposes no restrictions. The function is well-defined precisely when its denominator is nonzero:\n\n$$\nx-y+1\\ne 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ne x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y\\ne x+1\\}.\n$$\n\nPoints on either side of the line are allowed. Only the line $y=x+1$ is excluded."
