      ":question/id": "858ed249-4ab3-5d0f-9b2d-e746e5b3d1a4",
      ":question/problem": "Find the domain of the function $z=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y+2}}{x+y-1}$.",
      ":question/requires-calculator": false,
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which both conditions hold:\n\n- The expression under the square root is nonnegative.\n- The denominator is nonzero.\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+2&\\ge 0 &&\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ge -2,\\\\\nx+y-1&\\ne 0 &&\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y\\ne 1-x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y\\ge -2,\\ y\\ne 1-x\\}.\n$$\n\nA zero numerator is allowed, so the boundary $y=-2$ is included except at $(3,-2)$, where the denominator is zero. The denominator may be positive or negative."
    },
